   "subject": "Re: Shiny - input/output text with trema / diaeresis",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;m no expert on character encodings, but I suspect the problem is that Windows isn&#39;t displaying UTF-8 correctly. Character encoding in R is generally difficult on Windows.<div><br></div><div>The UTF-8 encoding for ë is c3 ab<span style=\"white-space:pre\"> in hex, which is equivalent to 195 171. If you try to print them </span><span style=\"white-space:pre\">out, you see different things on Mac and Windows (or at least, that&#39;s what I got):</span></div>\r\n\r\n<div>  rawToChar(as.raw(c(195, 171)))<br></div><div><div>  # [1] &quot;ë&quot;      # Mac</div><div>  # [1] &quot;Ã«&quot;     # Windows</div><div><br></div><div>You can use iconv to convert encodings. On Windows (for me), this prints out &quot;ë&quot;:</div>\r\n\r\n<div>  iconv(rawToChar(as.raw(c(195, 171))), from=&quot;UTF-8&quot;, to=&quot;ISO8859-1&quot;)<br></div></div><div>  # [1] &quot;ë&quot;</div><div><br></div><div>So maybe what&#39;s happening is that the values are sent over as UTF-8, and if you want them to display properly in Windows, you need to convert with iconv.</div>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div>-Winston</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Nov 8, 2013 at 8:57 AM, OB83 <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oliver....@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n\r\n</div></div>"
